<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance" xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema">
  <ScenTitle>Crossing the Line of Death (The War That Never Was) NATO, 1989</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;The two most powerful militaries in North Africa, Libya and Egypt, were not necessarily aligned with either NATO or the Warsaw Pact. Libya was theoretically a Soviet client state, but the Libyan dictator Qaddafi was notorious for placing his self-preservation above all else. Meanwhile, the United States spent many billions of dollars in aide to convince Egypt to join the western sphere of influence.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This scenario, based upon the book &lt;U&gt;The War That Never Was&lt;/U&gt; by Michael A. Palmer, begins with the neutrality of Libya and Egypt in doubt. Will Qaddafi side with the Soviets? Can the US cash in all those Egyptian I.O.U.s?&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This is a medium-sized scenario that features heavy air combat and coordinated air strikes.&lt;/P&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
